A||||15||MikeFD3S||Dorifto!  ||||10||
Z||000000||MikeFD3S||09-23-2001||11:16 PM||||Anyone in here into drifting?  I know it's pretty popular back in Japan, but theres always a few enthusiasts here and there.  Anyone ever take their Fiero out for some controlled slides?  Im wondering how the MR layout's weight distribution and suspension is set up.  The car definitely has enough torque to power over, but im not sure if it has the stability to maintain the drift.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif"> baka  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>-----------------------<BR>1993 Mazda RX7 R1<BR>A'pexi GT spec catback<BR>A'pexi Power Intake<BR>A'pexi PFC<BR>ACT Street Strip Clutch<BR>HKS Downpipe||||reg||10
Z||000001||Hank is Here||09-23-2001||11:33 PM||||Pardon my ignorance but what is "drifting"?<P>Any as to you MR2 question (not to be smart, but)  go to the MR2 forum and ask, they should know a little better than us.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||MikeFD3S||09-23-2001||11:42 PM||||When I said MR, It was pertaining to the Mid Engine, Rear wheel drive layout-- MR<P>Drifting is fun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Drifting is basically forcing the rear tires to lose traction, and causing the tail end to slide out...while entering small corrections with steering, throttle, or sidebrake.  Also known as throttle steer.  You'll enter a curve sideways, but will have a good exit angle once you're done with the turn.  Grip style driving is overall more efficient, but there are some instances where drifting into a turn will yield a better time.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Black88GT||09-23-2001||11:52 PM||||I've seen TV shows that have the WRX drifting, man it looks awesome, just like the ralley's....||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mach10||09-23-2001||11:53 PM||||Drifting works better with FWD or AWD... the MR layout is such that the rear has a little too much weight when accelerating, and too much momentum. Once is starts swinging, it tends to KEEP going. Very difficult to get it into a controlled "drift" I can't see that letting you gain time, cos you're bleeding speed working against the tires that way... Oversteer isn't so nice a position with these cars. Better off doing the conventional MR turn, into the curve cold, come out hot-hot-hot.||||reg||7
Z||000005||Hank is Here||09-24-2001||12:01 AM||||Sorry, guess I misunderstood you question at first.  Sounds like an interesting concept though I don't think I'll try it soon||||reg||1
Z||000006||Tigger||09-24-2001||12:12 AM||||Yes, Fieros are more likely to snap around due to the obvious weight distribution.  I've had my Fiero sideways a couple times.<P>1. Took an exit ramp corner with a "suggested" speed of 30mph at, oh say, around 45-50mph on wet pavement.  Yea, wasn't suprise it snapped.  Just rode it out, made the left turn - turning right, and gave those behind me a good show.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>2. Just last week, also on wet pavement.  Made a hard left turn, let the rear kick out, shifted into 2nd, kicked the rear out some more and powered away.<P>I'd have to agree, drifting is fun but should only be done by professional drivers on a closed circuit course.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 09-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||MikeFD3S||09-24-2001||03:32 AM||||actually, RWD is the best platform for drifting and FWD is the worst.  We aptly call FWD drifting 'ass dragging' since thats all it is.  In a good drift, hardly any speed will be bled off...it's kind of like flying<P>I'm figuring the weight distribution on the Fiero is like 40/60... if there was some way to put a lightweight 13b in a Fiero, it should even out the weight perfectly<P>I've seen people drift Mr2s so drifting a Fiero shouldnt be any diff... just lots of practice...thats what auto-x is for  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  That is...if you don't get kicked off the track<P>Heres a cool video of Best Motoring in Japan drifting a 3rd gen through multiple curves, with 3 views (one of the pedals, one of the steering wheel, and one exterior shot)<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fxracer.com/videos/drift2/rx7drift.mpeg" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fxracer.com/videos/drift2/rx7drift.mpeg</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||09-24-2001||03:43 AM||||Weight distribution is more like 47/53.  Drifting in a RWD car may be good (in a race), but not necessarily with a mid engine.  Too much weight back there.  (Although drifting has it's advantages when trying to turn a corner in the snow.  Get started in the corner, mash the gas and go.)||||reg||1
Z||000009||MikeFD3S||09-24-2001||02:44 PM||||no apology needed hank =P   I wish it snowed on occasion in Los Angeles, the most we have to to look forward to the equally rare shower||||reg||1
Z||000010||fiero56||09-24-2001||06:31 PM||||I drift on wet pavement all the time. Scares the $hit out of my passengers until they get used to it, then when I don't, they ask why I didn't(usually there is oncoming traffic or something, I try to be a little bit safe). I've only lost it once, that was in snow, I ended up in someones front yard with my Formula at 7am.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Ed||09-24-2001||08:09 PM||||I was ENCOURAGED to drift by an instructor at SCCA's Thunderhill driving school.  Yep, the Fiero will four wheel drift, even in the dry.  Of course, this was on a closed course, under the supervision of a "professional."<P>Keep those revs up!||||reg||1
Z||000012||Kasdan||09-24-2001||09:48 PM||||I drift quite often...only lost it once, ended in the wrong lane facing the onecoming traffic...<P>------------------<BR>Tyler Morgan, 1986 GT<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero21.jpg">         <BR>Power Steering, Holley Scoop<BR>Aim=FieroDriver1986||||reg||1
Z||000013||ray b||09-25-2001||12:11 AM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Monkeyman:<BR><B>Weight distribution is more like 47/53.  Drifting in a RWD car may be good (in a race), but not necessarily with a mid engine.  Too much weight back there.  (Although drifting has it's advantages when trying to turn a corner in the snow.  Get started in the corner, mash the gas and go.)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>47/53????? WTF NO WAY closer to 40/60 0r more rear weight with the v6 and even a duke is near or over 40/60 unless major mods.<BR>but sure will drift controling drift is hard to learn save your fiero and learn on your computer with gpl sim.<BR>GPL SIM is great model of mid-eng 67 gp cars and a good way to learn to drift/spin/slide without kill yourself free demo down loads at<P>GRAND PRIX LEGENDS <A HREF="http://www.papy.com/gpl/gpl.download.index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.papy.com/gpl/gpl.downloads.index.html</A> <BR>  or papy's site or do a web search for grand prix legends web ring<P>game CD is on sale at $5 to 20 at discount stores.<BR>it is not a eazy hero driver game, hard to learn. but very real feel to it<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd<P>[This message has been edited by ray b (edited 09-25-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by ray b (edited 09-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||MikeFD3S||09-25-2001||04:11 AM||||the fine line of drifting is when the rear tires are spinning but still getting a tiny bit of traction...with the weight of the rear, if it gets too much traction, it will snap the car the opposite way and you'll do 360's or at least a 180<P>You had a cool instructor  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">... most places kick you off for drifting on the course||||reg||1
Z||000015||danny boy||09-25-2001||07:00 PM||||As much as I can. I set the GT up to get squirrley when I want it to. I think Fieros are a little too short to be really effective though. they kick too fast without enough steering control. 4 wheel drift and wet weather fun isnt the same as what is you're talking about. ||||reg||1
